\details{
\strong{Economic intuition and pitfalls.} \code{kQ} encodes how fast the transmission mechanism (lag
coefficients) is believed to move. With \code{kQ = 0.01} the prior says a coefficient drifts by about 1\% of
its OLS standard error per quarter: over 170 quarters that is a standard deviation of roughly 13\%
of the standard error times \eqn{\sqrt{170}}, so substantial but gradual change is allowed. Raising
\code{kQ} lets the data attribute any misfit (outliers, omitted variables, mis-specified lags) to
coefficient drift, i.e. \emph{spurious} time variation. The prior is not neutral: with an
inverse-Wishart prior whose degrees of freedom are tied to a short training sample, the posterior of \code{Q}
is dominated by the prior in samples of typical macro length. Always report results for several
\code{kQ} values and compare with the constant-coefficient fit.
